                <pb facs="0461" n="435" sameAs="frus:frus1955-57v11/pg435"/>

                <head>206. Telegram From the Department of State to the
                        Embassy in the Republic of China<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d206-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                ><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                                when="1955-12-06T20:06:00-05:00">December 6, 1955—8:06
                            p.m.</date></hi></dateline></opener>
                <p>340. Your 516. Transmit following message from Secretary to President Chiang:</p>
                <p>“The President has discussed with me your reply of December 3 to his second
                        letter<anchor n="2" xml:id="d206-f2"/> regarding pending candidacy of 18
                    countries for membership in the United Nations. The President has asked me to
                    inform you of his disappointment and profound concern at the continued prospect
                    of your use of the veto in the impending Security Council vote on the membership
                    question.</p>
                <p>“A question is raised in the second paragraph of your letter as to whether
                    something more might be done to have the application of Outer Mongolia dropped
                    or its consideration technically postponed. The possibility of such a solution
                    has been fully explored by U.S. representatives to no avail. While no one can
                    predict with confidence the course of events in the Security Council when this
                    item comes up, there is every indication that no maneuver along this line would
                    be successful.</p>
                <p>“It is our considered judgment that a Chinese veto on this membership issue would
                    play squarely into the hands of our worst enemies and jeopardize the position we
                    have jointly striven to maintain.”</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold"
                            >Dulles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
